9825 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479825 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479825 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186479686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479825 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ff4a0306-bebb-5a0b-88e8-580bcd8a3741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479833 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479833 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65128" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479833 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479833 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$\n\nII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x{e}^{xy}$\n\nIII. ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2y+1$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2y+1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479833 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0c2a62d8-6d93-5394-bb23-b474c6edf899" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479833 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy} \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}(1+xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y+1)=2 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481568 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186481675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481568 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186481716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481568 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186481724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481568 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186481739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481568 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186481744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481568 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481568 #edb/ref 1045 "Equality of Mixed Partial Derivatives" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481568 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186481716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481568 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481568 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481568 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "3c12d8df-59d7-51f9-9588-92c3d42e2238" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481675 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481675 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0330742a-33ff-5afc-89cc-0f92fa6fe78f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481675 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186479833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481716 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186481675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481716 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f5bbb0a-88b6-5f7d-9f06-8a26f6ee53a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186481716 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17